{"id":"1df911af-65a0-4e7f-9324-e75904f9715f","year":"2026-2027","code":"CFBGR05905","facultyCode":"cf","facultyTitle":{"en":"Campus Fryslân","nl":"Campus Fryslân"},"title":{"en":"Information Technology for Interdisciplinary Studies","nl":""},"fullTitle":{"en":"Information Technology for Interdisciplinary Studies (Foundation)","nl":""},"goals":{"en":"Upon the successful completion of this course, students will be able to:\n-\tDevelop essential digital literacy skills, acquire IT methods for scientific research, and explore the invaluable role of technology in the social sciences and humanities.\n-\tGet an overview of the current digital landscape, including data management, web technologies, and the current developments in AI\n-\tDistinguish and use different data types and file formats\n-\tLearn basic programming skills in Python\n-\tGather insights and elemental skills on IT tools for Earth System Science","nl":""},"description":{"en":"Information Technology (IT) for Interdisciplinary Studies combines digital literacy, responsible use of artificial intelligence, programming, data analysis, and the practical use of technology in the social and natural sciences.\n\nPart 1 (weeks 1-3) - Foundations of Digital Literacy and AI. Students will explore how the internet, websites, and personal computers handle data. They will learn how web addresses, DNS, HTTPS, cookies, permissions, file systems, deletion and backups work, and will use the principles of confidentiality, integrity, and availability to understand common digital risks. This section also covers phishing, password security, and authentication. Students will then develop a practical understanding of how large language models generate answers, why their output may be unreliable, what information should not be shared with AI systems, and how prompt injection and other AI-related security risks arise.\n\nPart 2 (weeks 3-6) - Introduction to programming: This section equips students with introductory programming skills in the Programming language Python. Students will be equipped with tools to read, edit, and manipulate data. \n\nPart 3 (weeks 6-7) - IT applicable to scientific research in various fields from the Earth System Sciences (ESS) (including disciplines from climate science, ecology, and geography) for the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs). Participants will learn about selected tools and services such as QGIS for Geographic Information Systems (GIS) and time series data enabling them to analyze spatial and temporal data effectively. Additionally, they will be introduced to case studies from various fields to understand how technology enhances research methodologies in these domains. Students will apply their skills in week 7 in a group presentation. \n\n\n\n","nl":""},"credits":5.00,"hours":4,"levels":["BACHELOR"],"formats":[],"formatsExplanation":{"en":"","nl":""},"assessmentsExplanation":{"en":"","nl":""},"courseTypes":[{"id":"4920dc06-f7b5-493e-a3e1-cb4ad8d4c5d5","position":0,"optionList":{"id":"639c4fba-cddd-4593-acb5-5c11b667a363","textEn":"Bachelor","textNl":"Bachelor","position":1,"deletedSince":null,"availableSince":null}}],"prerequisites":{"en":"Admission to the Bachelor’s degree programme of Global Responsibility & Leadership.","nl":""},"draft":false,"otherPeriods":false,"organizationalUnit":"71453b8c-d842-40cc-b6d5-e9b6e31ec5ca","organizationalUnitTitle":{"en":"Org Campus Fryslân ","nl":"Org Campus Fryslân"},"startYear":null,"endYear":null,"legacyAssessments":{"en":"","nl":""},"legacyCourseTypes":{"en":"","nl":""},"legacyFormats":{"en":"","nl":""},"legacyTermExplanationEn":"","legacyTermExplanationNl":"","enlight":"NOT_ENLIGHT","modifiedAt":[2026,8,19,12,49,27,846852000],"courseOfferings":[{"id":"62d0a9f4-91d5-42c8-a65a-7079d2ae7280","offeringCode":"CFBGR05905.2026-2027.1","scheduleLink":"","staff":[{"id":"c3388460-bee1-461a-87fb-9562cde92215","username":"","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Marchenko","titlePrefix":"","titleSuffix":"MSc","initials":"I."